Array Memory Representation in Java
      In Java, arrays are stored in memory with the following characteristics:

Contiguous Memory Allocation: Arrays occupy a continuous block of memory where elements are stored sequentially.
Reference Type: In Java, an array variable is a reference variable that points to an object containing the array elements.
Memory Structure:

The array reference (4 bytes on 32-bit JVM, 8 bytes on 64-bit JVM) points to the array object in heap memory
The array object includes:

A header with type information and length (typically 12-16 bytes)
The actual array data




Element Storage:

      Primitive arrays (int[], char[], etc.) store values directly
      Reference arrays (String[], Object[], etc.) store references to objects, not the objects themselves


Addressing: Java uses zero-based indexing. Each element can be accessed using the base address plus an offset: baseAddress + (index * elementSize)
Memory Usage Example:

int[] array (4 bytes per element): An array of 100 integers uses approximately 416 bytes (16-byte header + 100 × 4 bytes)
Object[] array: An array of 100 object references uses approximately 416-816 bytes (depending on JVM), plus the memory for each referenced object



Static vs Dynamic Arrays
      Static Arrays (Traditional Java Arrays)

      Fixed Size: Size must be specified at creation time and cannot be changed afterward

javaCopyint[] numbers = new int[10]; // Size fixed at 10 elements

Memory Allocation: All memory is allocated at once during creation
Performance:

O(1) time complexity for access, insertion, and deletion (at known positions)
No overhead for resizing


Limitations:

Cannot grow or shrink after creation
Must handle index out of bounds manually
Wasted memory if array is larger than needed



Dynamic Arrays (Implemented by ArrayList in Java)

Flexible Size: Can grow and shrink as needed
javaCopyArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>(); // No fixed size

Internal Implementation: Uses a backing array that is resized automatically
Growth Strategy: When capacity is reached, a new larger array is created (typically 1.5x current size in Java), and elements are copied over
Performance:

O(1) amortized time complexity for adding elements
O(n) time complexity for resizing operations
Extra memory overhead for capacity management


Advantages:

Automatically handles capacity
More convenient API for common operations



Java's Array Utility Methods (Arrays Class)
The java.util.Arrays class provides powerful utilities for working with arrays:

Searching:

Arrays.binarySearch(array, key): Finds element in a sorted array using binary search

javaCopyint index = Arrays.binarySearch(sortedArray, 42);

Sorting:

Arrays.sort(array): Sorts array elements (uses dual-pivot quicksort for primitives)
Arrays.sort(array, comparator): Sorts using custom comparator
Arrays.parallelSort(array): Parallel sorting for large arrays

javaCopyArrays.sort(numbers);
Arrays.sort(employees, Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary));

Filling/Setting Values:

Arrays.fill(array, value): Fills entire array with specified value
Arrays.fill(array, fromIndex, toIndex, value): Fills portion of array

javaCopyArrays.fill(numbers, 0);  // Reset all elements to zero

Equality and Comparison:

Arrays.equals(array1, array2): Checks element-by-element equality
Arrays.deepEquals(array1, array2): For multi-dimensional arrays

javaCopyboolean areEqual = Arrays.equals(arr1, arr2);

Copying:

Arrays.copyOf(array, newLength): Creates copy with specified length
Arrays.copyOfRange(array, fromIndex, toIndex): Copies portion

javaCopyint[] copy = Arrays.copyOf(original, original.length);

Converting:

Arrays.asList(array): Converts array to fixed-size List
Arrays.stream(array): Converts to stream for functional operations

javaCopyList<String> list = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "c");
IntStream stream = Arrays.stream(numbers);

String Representation:

Arrays.toString(array): Returns string representation
Arrays.deepToString(array): For multi-dimensional arrays

javaCopySystem.out.println(Arrays.toString(numbers));


ArrayList vs Arrays in Java
Arrays

Fixed Size: Cannot be resized after creation
Performance: Slightly better performance and memory usage
Syntax: Uses square bracket notation
javaCopyint[] numbers = new int[10];
numbers[0] = 42;

Type Restrictions: Can store primitives (int, char, etc.) directly
Limitations:

Basic functionality only
No built-in methods for adding/removing elements
Must manage size manually



ArrayList

Dynamic Size: Automatically resizes as needed
javaCopyArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add(42); // Grows as needed

Generic Type: Uses generics, can't store primitives directly (requires wrapper classes)
javaCopyArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(); // Not int, but Integer

Rich API: Many useful methods

add(), remove(), get(), set()
contains(), indexOf(), clear()
addAll(), removeAll(), subList()


Overhead: Slightly more memory overhead and potentially slower for very performance-critical applications
Advantages:

Much more flexibility and convenience
Handles resizing automatically
Part of Collections framework, integrates with other collections



When to Use Each
Use Arrays when:

Size is fixed and known in advance
Working with primitive types for maximum performance
Memory usage is critical
Direct access to memory is needed
Multi-dimensional data structures are required
Very performance-sensitive code

Use ArrayList when:

Size may change during program execution
Need convenience methods for adding/removing elements
Want to leverage Collections framework features
Code readability and maintenance is more important than absolute performance
Working with objects rather than primitives
